  "preview": "Two rectangles. One is 2x by (3x + 5). The other is 5 by (4x + 12). Same area when? And what does that even look like?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use the distributive property to write equivalent expressions for the area of rectangles, evaluate them, and reason backward to find when two area expressions are equal.",
    "studentPrompt": "Two rectangles are shown.\n\n- Rectangle A has width 2x and length (3x + 5).\n- Rectangle B has width 5 and length (4x + 12).\n\n1. If x = 3, find the area of each rectangle. Which is greater?\n2. Now treat x as unknown. Write a simplified expression for the area of each rectangle (no parentheses).\n3. For what value of x do the two rectangles have the same area?\n4. For what value of x is Rectangle A's area exactly twice Rectangle B's area?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/35466647/pexels-photo-35466647.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Ornate vintage tiles with intricate black floral designs indoors.",
      "credit": "Photo by Magda Ehlers on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the two rectangles. Read the dimensions. Tell groups: 'Try x = 3 first, then decide whether you can find a value of x where the two areas are equal.' Random groups, vertical whiteboards. Don't pre-teach the distributive property — let them rediscover it.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "expressions",
      "distributive-property",
      "combining-like-terms",
      "area",
      "equations",
      "evaluating"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Layered task. Q1 is plug-and-chug (low floor — multiply numbers). Q2 is the diamond: distributing 2x over (3x + 5) introduces a quadratic term, which surprises many groups. Q3 demands an equation with x and x squared, opening the door to factoring or graphing. Q4 reverses direction and produces an interesting algebraic surprise.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: At x = 3, Rectangle A = 2(3) × (3(3) + 5) = 6 × 14 = 84. Rectangle B = 5 × (4(3) + 12) = 5 × 24 = 120. B is greater.\n- Q2: Rectangle A = 2x(3x + 5) = 6x^2 + 10x. Rectangle B = 5(4x + 12) = 20x + 60.\n- Q3: Set 6x^2 + 10x = 20x + 60. Rearrange: 6x^2 - 10x - 60 = 0, or 3x^2 - 5x - 30 = 0. Using the quadratic formula or estimation: x ≈ 4.05. (Students may estimate by trying values: at x = 4, A = 96 + 40 = 136, B = 80 + 60 = 140 — very close. At x = 4.1, A ≈ 141.86, B = 142.) For grade 7, accept 'between 4 and 5' or 'about 4.05.'\n- Q4: Set 6x^2 + 10x = 2(20x + 60) = 40x + 120. Rearrange: 6x^2 - 30x - 120 = 0, or x^2 - 5x - 20 = 0. By estimation, x ≈ 7.62. (Acceptable: 'between 7 and 8.')",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Multiplying 2x × (3x + 5) and getting 6x + 5 — forgetting to distribute and forgetting that x · x = x^2.\n- Computing perimeter instead of area (adding sides instead of multiplying).\n- For Q3, ignoring the x^2 term and solving 10x = 20x + 60 → x = -6 (wrong; this skips the leading term).\n- For Q4, doubling Rectangle A's area instead of Rectangle B's — answering the wrong question.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- For Q2, what did you do with the 2x times 3x? How did you know it became 6x squared?\n- In Q3, the answer isn't a nice whole number. How did your group narrow in on it?\n- Q4 asks when one rectangle is twice as large as the other. Did the answer surprise you — was it about twice as big as Q3's answer? Why or why not?",
    "extensions": "- For what value of x is Rectangle A's PERIMETER equal to Rectangle B's PERIMETER? (This produces a linear equation — nicer numbers.)\n- Sketch Rectangle A and Rectangle B as accurately as you can for x = 1, x = 3, and x = 5. What changes about their shapes as x grows? Which one 'wins' the area race in the long run, and why?\n- Design your own pair of rectangles with algebraic dimensions whose areas are equal at exactly x = 2 (and no other positive x). Is that possible?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. At x = 3: Rectangle A = 6 × 14 = 84 sq units. Rectangle B = 5 × 24 = 120 sq units. B is greater by 36.\n2. Rectangle A: 2x(3x + 5) = 6x^2 + 10x. Rectangle B: 5(4x + 12) = 20x + 60.\n3. 6x^2 + 10x = 20x + 60 → 6x^2 - 10x - 60 = 0 → 3x^2 - 5x - 30 = 0. Quadratic formula: x = (5 ± √(25 + 360)) / 6 = (5 ± √385) / 6. Positive root ≈ (5 + 19.62)/6 ≈ 4.10. Accept 'about 4.1' or 'between 4 and 5.'\n4. 6x^2 + 10x = 2(20x + 60) = 40x + 120 → 6x^2 - 30x - 120 = 0 → x^2 - 5x - 20 = 0. Positive root x = (5 + √105)/2 ≈ 7.62. Accept 'about 7.6' or 'between 7 and 8.'",
